  abstract={Testing modern software systems, especially those with graphical user interfaces, remains a significant challenge, with advanced automation techniques struggling to achieve comprehensive coverage. End users regularly encounter real-world bugs that remain invisible to formal testing tools. This paper introduces a novel, gamified framework for transforming casual bug reports into executable UI tests. Our system leverages large language models to convert natural language descriptions into a domain-specific testing language (DSL), which is then automatically executed using browser automation. Users are incentivized through points, badges, and progression mechanics to submit clear, actionable reports, turning test generation into an engaging and collaborative process. Unlike traditional testing pipelines, our approach is modular, locally deployable, and designed for scalability through user participation. By tightly integrating gamification into the test execution loop, we not only validate bugs but reshape how coverage is expanded through creativity, competition, and the untapped potential of real users.},
